## TL;DR

The **5 mistakes** buyers make with a three stone asscher cut engagement ring:

1. **Using round side stones** — round brilliants next to a square step-cut create a shape conflict. Baguette, trapezoid, or half-moon side stones follow the asscher's geometry.
2. **Buying SI clarity on the center** — the step-cut Hall of Mirrors effect shows every inclusion. VS1 minimum for the center stone, always.
3. **Making all three stones equal size** — equal sizing looks like a wide band from a distance. The center should be 50–60% of total ring carat weight to create visual hierarchy.
4. **Ignoring side stone clarity** — baguette and trapezoid side stones are step-cuts too. SI2 side stones next to a VS1 center are visibly inconsistent.
5. **Overbuilding the setting, underbuilding the stone** — a $10,000 Gallery Collection frame around a 1ct SI2 center is backwards. The stone drives the ring.

→ Best entry combo: [GIA 1.00ct E-VS1 at $2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) + [Bezel Straight Baguette WG at $2,302](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/bezel-straight-baguette-cut-diamond-three-stone-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-316318?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) = **$4,766 total**.

**Priority:** Choose the center stone first. Lock in VS1 clarity and E–F color. Then pick the side stone shape that matches the asscher's square geometry.

