**What this means for you:** The [1.52ct D-IF lab at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) paired with the [Bezel Baguette WG at $2,302](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/bezel-straight-baguette-cut-diamond-three-stone-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-316318?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) creates a $5,097 ring with a 1.52ct D-IF center — a substantially larger stone than the GIA entry combination at nearly the same price.

> **Farzana's take:** The three-stone format is where lab diamonds make the strongest argument. The scale effect means a 1.84ct lab center in a three-stone baguette setting looks like a 2.2–2.3ct solitaire to the naked eye. I have shown that combination to clients who budget $6,000 total and they walk out with a ring that reads like a $15,000 solitaire. It's the most efficient use of a lab stone's size advantage.

## Clarity and Color for the Three Stone Format

For the complete [asscher cut diamond engagement ring](/asscher-cut-diamond-engagement-rings) family, clarity is the dominant concern. In a three-stone build it affects all three stones.

**Center stone:** VS1 minimum. This is non-negotiable for a step-cut. An SI1 inclusion in an asscher center reflects in every facet simultaneously.

**Side stones:** VS1–VS2 preferred. If the side stones are baguette or trapezoid (step-cut), treat their clarity seriously — they have the same mirror-like transparency as the center. If the side stones are half-moon (which has more faceting), VS2 is more forgiving.

**Color:** E or F for platinum and white gold settings. G works for yellow gold and rose gold settings because the warm metal tone offsets any slight warmth in the stone.

**Important note on side stone color matching:** The side stones should match or be one grade warmer than the center. If you buy an E-color center stone, use E or F side stones. A G-color side stone next to an E center will look visibly warmer — this is one of the most common and most avoidable mistakes in three-stone builds.

## Sizing the Side Stones Right

The ideal center-to-total-carat ratio for a three-stone asscher ring is 50–60% center. This is the range where the ring looks like a featured center stone with companions rather than three equal stones.

**For a $5,000–$7,000 ring budget:**
- Target a 0.90–1.10ct center with 0.35–0.50ct total side stones
- The [1ct E-VS1 center](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) paired with the [Tapered Baguette 1/4ct WG](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/three-stone-tapered-baguette-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-1-4-ct-tw-item-203640?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) creates this ratio at $5,021 total

**For a $7,500–$10,000 ring budget:**
- Target a 1.00–1.20ct center with 0.50ct total side stones
- The [1ct E-VS1 center](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with [Trapezoid Platinum ($5,167)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/three-stone-trapezoid-sidestone-diamond-engagement-ring-in-platinum-1-2-ct-tw-item-192941?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) hits this range at $7,631 total

**For $10,000+ ring budget:**
