## How to Choose Between Baguette, Trapezoid, and Half-Moon

All three are compatible with an asscher center. The choice comes down to what look you want:

**Baguette:** The most geometric option. Straight or tapered rectangular stones sit parallel to the center stone's sides. The result is a fully rectangular, Art Deco-influenced ring. Clean, modern, and the most common professional choice for three-stone step-cut builds.

**Trapezoid:** More formal than baguette. The tapered four-sided shape creates a precise geometric connection between center and band. Trapezoid settings cost more than baguette settings at the same quality because they require more precise calibration. The look is definitively architectural.

**Half-moon:** The most romantic of the three options. Semicircular stones hug the sides of the asscher. They soften the edges without introducing round-brilliant sparkle. Good for buyers who want a classic three-stone look without committing fully to the Art Deco step-cut aesthetic.

Choose based on the personality of the person wearing it. Geometric and structured: baguette or trapezoid. Romantic and classic: half-moon.

## How a Three Stone Asscher Ring Wears Daily

A three-stone setting has more prongs than a solitaire. That means more maintenance checkpoints. Farzana recommends a prong inspection every 12–18 months for any three-stone ring worn daily — the side stone prongs are smallest and wear first.

Baguette and trapezoid side stones have exposed corners. These corners are the most vulnerable point on any step-cut stone. A sharp impact on a hard surface can chip a corner.

The risk is not high for daily wear, but it is real — buyers who work with their hands should ask about bezel-set baguette options, which wrap the stone's edge in metal.

Step-cut side stones collect oil and soap film faster than round brilliants because their large flat facets act like windows — residue is immediately visible. Clean a three-stone asscher ring with a soft brush and warm soapy water every two weeks. Ultrasonic cleaners are safe for prong-set settings with VS1+ clarity stones.

The ring looks different from different angles. Face-on, the three stones create a wide, architecturally striking profile. From the side, the ring is thinner than it looks from above — the asscher's depth sits below the finger line.

Buyers expecting a tall profile from the side should choose a setting with a higher basket, which lifts the center stone above the band.

A three-stone ring is heavier than a solitaire of the same center stone size because of the additional metal and stones. A 1ct center with 0.50ct total side stones in a platinum setting weighs noticeably more than a 1ct solitaire. This is not a problem — it is a quality indicator.

The weight tells you the setting is solid metal, not thin-cast. Platinum three-stone settings feel the most substantial in hand — that weight is part of the appeal for buyers who want a ring that feels as serious as it looks.

The profile when the hand is held flat is the view most people see most often. In that view, the three-stone asscher shows the full width of the ring — all three stones visible, all lines aligned. This is why correct stone sizing matters so much.

The 50–60% center ratio ensures the center stone dominates that view rather than blending into an undifferentiated row.

